            "constraintComplexity": "High — simultaneous contacts and grasp forces",
            "constraintChange": "Moderate — contacts shift without release",
            "motionRegime": "Quasistatic"
          },
          "shortDescription": "Change the orientation of a small rectangular prism from a stable grasp to another stable grasp.",
          "configurationImages": [
            { "src": "Images/task_pictures/in_hand_reorientation/starting.jpg", "caption": "Initial grasp", "alt": "Stock reference of a cube held in an initial grasp" },
            { "src": "Images/task_pictures/in_hand_reorientation/ending.jpg", "caption": "Target grasp", "alt": "Stock reference of a cube held in a different orientation" }
          ],
          "description": "Change the orientation of a small rectangular prism from a stable grasp at any orientation relative to the palm to a stable grasp at any other orientation.",
          "justification": "In-Hand Reorienting evaluates a system's ability to manipulate an object within the hand while maintaining continuous control and without engaging the environment. The task involves quasistatic direct manipulation of a rigid object whose dimensions are comparable to those of the hand. Coordinating the object's pose with multiple finger joints and contacts produces high controlled degrees of freedom. The task also exhibits high constraint complexity because successful completion depends on coordinating simultaneous contacts, regulating grasp forces, and maintaining a precise object orientation. Constraint change is moderate because contacts may shift within the hand while the object remains continuously controlled and no environmental interface is introduced. More broadly, in-hand reorientation is a hallmark capability of dexterous manipulation and a canonical benchmark of multifingered robotic hands.",
          "items": [
            {
              "item": "Rectangular prism",
              "quantity": 1,
              "specification": "100 mm × 50 mm × 25 mm",
