          ],
          "description": "Spin a top so that it remains upright.",
          "justification": "Spin a Top evaluates a system's ability to exploit object dynamics to achieve a desired outcome. The task involves dynamic direct manipulation of one rigid object that is smaller than the hand within a rigid environment, producing low controlled degrees of freedom. It also has low constraint complexity because few environmental constraints are active simultaneously. Constraint change is moderate, consisting primarily of grasping the top, releasing it, and establishing contact with the table. Unlike quasistatic benchmarks, success depends critically on transferring momentum and generating the appropriate orientation, angular velocity, and release conditions. Because the top's stability depends on the imparted spin, small errors in orientation, velocity, or timing can lead to immediate failure. More broadly, spinning a top represents dynamic dexterous manipulation in which physical dynamics are intentionally exploited.",
